>> title: Soy Sauce Chicken, Slow Cooker
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: chicken, chinese, soy sauce, poached, whole bird, slow cooker, crock pot
>> counters: The Slow Cooker
>> dish: soy-sauce-chicken
>> kit: Slow Cooker
>> aka: see yao gai, si yau gai, 豉油雞, soya chicken, soy sauce chicken, lou sui gai, slow cooker soy sauce chicken, crockpot poached chicken
>> servings: 4
>> time: 4 hr 40 min
>> slack: unforgiving — the breast goes dry and stringy in the same pot that made the thighs perfect, and there is no reversing it
>> step.2: simmer the lou sui 20 min on the stove
>> step.3: poach on low, 4 hr
>> step.4: rest 20 min, brush with sesame oil, chop

The shortest cook on the shelf, at four hours, and the only one where longer is actively worse. A master-stock poach wants a liquid that never boils, and a slow cooker on low is closer to that than a burner is — much closer than pressure, which tightens a whole bird. Nothing is browned and nothing may be: colour on this chicken comes from dark soy, not from heat. The stock is built on the stove first and goes into the crock hot, which is both the plain recipe's own first step and the reason the bird is not sitting for hours climbing slowly out of cold.

Simmer @water{5%cups}(1.2 L) with @light soy sauce{1 1/2%cups}(360 mL), @dark soy sauce{1/2%cup}(120 mL), @Shaoxing wine{1/2%cup}(120 mL), @rock sugar{4%oz}(115 g), @star anise{3%pods}, @cassia bark{1%stick}, @dried tangerine peel{1%piece}, @fresh ginger{6%slices}(coin-sized), @scallions{3}(knotted) in a #saucepan{} for ~simmer{20%min}, until the sugar has gone and the spices have opened.

Slow cook @whole chicken{3 1/2%lb}(1.6 kg; rinsed and dried) breast-down in @&(~1)master stock{}, poured over it hot in a #slow cooker{}, on low for ~slow cook{4%hr}, the bird submerged to the shoulders and the lid on. Lower it in by the neck and let the cavity fill, or it floats and cooks unevenly. Four hours, not six: the thigh is done long before the breast gives up.

Rest @&(~1)chicken{} in the pot switched off for ~rest{20%min}, lift it out to drain, brush it with @toasted sesame oil{2%tsp}, and chop it through the bone. Strain the lou sui rather than finishing it — a master stock is kept: chill it, lift the cap of fat off, and boil it up on the stove every few days, topping up the soy and the sugar. A crock cannot boil, so the keeping of the stock never happens in it.
